Perfect. I now have the DOI/link document and I can already see the major issue:

You currently have two competing publication systems on the page:

The large detailed publication lists inside the Research Areas accordions
The separate “Selected Publications” section lower down

That duplication is making the site feel bloated and academically messy.

Here’s the cleaner direction I’d strongly recommend:

Keep the detailed publication references inside each research area
Completely redesign the standalone Publications section into:
curated/high-impact featured publications
professionally formatted citation cards
DOI buttons
journal/year metadata
cleaner hierarchy
less scrolling fatigue
visually academic rather than “developer list”

And now that I have the DOI file, we can also:

fix broken links
make every paper clickable properly
normalize citation formatting
make the section feel closer to:
Stanford lab pages
Broad Institute
Nature lab sites
UHN / ICES style publication archives

You’re actually pretty close now. The CSS foundation is already much better than before. The weak point is mainly the publication presentation layer itself.

ok please give me the code to fix that section

I’ll give you this as copy-paste blocks: first replace the current publication CSS, then replace the whole <section id="publications"...> block. This keeps the fix contained so GitHub doesn’t turn into a raccoon in a keyboard factory.

Yes. Use this full replacement for the Publications section. It uses the DOI file you uploaded as the source list.

1. Replace your current publication CSS

Replace the CSS starting at:

.publication-groups {

down to the end of:

.pub-doi {

with this:

.publication-toolbar {
